e  bit in the last two years or three years  as well so to sort of suddenly be a  co-instructor of the class with grace  was kind of a sudden almost kind of  jarring kind of experience a baptism of  fire  perhaps so maybe there's there's there's  three things to say one is it was you  know immensely challenging it is  different from doing research which is  mostly when I'm doing as a PhD student  even you know working on projects with  different people you sort of have this  kind of one level remove where suddenly  as grace is talking about you know  working with students and trying to  encourage them in certain directions and  there's certainly some uncertainty  associated with that a whether they will  take your advice I guess or whether you  should nudge them further in this  direction or sometimes they surprise you  or you know very often frankly they  surprise you very pleasantly in terms of  what they're able to accomplish over the  semester so how to kind of inspire or  mentor students is really something that  that that I learned over the semester I  think a part of this is that it was it  was pretty emotional i guess i think  maybe that's the right word in terms of  the kind of the history of this class  the very personal nature of it i think  we got to know you know our 35 students  very well over the semester and  certainly the clients as well the 11  clients that we worked with and so you  really kind of experience the ups and  downs with with the students over the  course of the projects and that's you  know I think challenging sometimes or  you know interesting to experience  I think the third thing I'd say is that  it was it was it was in the end very  rewarding I mean to see the results and  see students excited about what they had  done to be able to work with a really  great team of people all passionate  about the course so the co instructors  and you know our teaching and lab  assistants who had taken the class  before and really wanted to make t